It makes sense to want a practical way to protect yourself, especially if you’ve repeatedly faced caste-coded condescension from people with authority over you.

A useful “heads-up” is to assess the **setting and behavior**, not assume the conclusion from caste alone. For example, watch for:

- insistence on deference or unsolicited moral/intellectual authority;
- caste questions, surname probing, marriage or food “purity” talk;
- exclusion from social or religious activities;
- dismissing lower-caste colleagues’ expertise or experience;
- unequal standards, jokes, slurs, or favoritism through caste networks.

With a Brahmin boss or in-law, you can keep boundaries and document concrete workplace conduct where needed. In a work setting, focus on observable actions—credit-taking, unequal assignments, casteist remarks, retaliation, exclusion—and use HR, a union, trusted senior colleagues, or relevant legal/anti-discrimination channels if it becomes serious. With family, it may help to establish limits early: “I won’t participate in caste-based comments or rituals that rank people,” or “Please don’t ask about or discuss my caste.”

That approach doesn’t require ignoring caste power dynamics; it avoids treating every person from a birth group as certain to behave badly before they do.
